ormat explanation: 'Rank: Count', e.g., '2: 4' means there are four 2s left unknown)\nBlackJoker: 1, 2: 1, A: 1, K: 2, Q: 1, 10: 1, 9: 1, 4: 2, 3: 2\nValid Moves:\nsingle: ♦5\nsingle: ♥7\nsingle: ♥8\nsingle: ♠9\nsingle: ♥10\nsingle: ♦J\nsingle: ♣Q\nsingle: ♦K\nsingle: ♥A\nsingle: RedJoker\npass: []\n\nSelect the best move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': '**Analysis of a critical play in the game of Dou Dizhu.**\n\nMy mind is racing. I\'m a peasant in this game, and my teammate is on the cusp of victory. They have only four cards left, and their recent play of a single 4 card suggests they\'re either trying to set me up, or desperately trying to get rid of a card. I need to figure out the Landlord\'s (who has 8 cards left) possible moves, and how I can help my teammate win.\n\nLooking back at the game history is always a good starting point. The Landlord played some high cards earlier, maybe a bomb of 2s, but the log notation is strange, so I am unsure. My teammate\'s been aggressive with pairs, which leads me to believe they might still be holding some. My own hand is strong, but a bit unwieldy at seventeen cards. I\'ve got a mix of high cards and pairs, a potential straight, and the Red Joker! But it seems unwise to go too aggro if my teammate has a specific strategy, and my role is more to support them.\n\nThe crucial detail: my teammate has just led with a single 4 card, and the Landlord passed. This feels like a set-up. My first instinct is to play a high card to force the Landlord to use their resources. However, I need to consider the play order. The log says it is "my turn". The notation of the previous player (Teammate) is "4". So, the correct order is: My Teammate, then Me, then the Landlord. This means I play before the Landlord. In this case, I absolutely must protect my teammate and play to the right of the turn. So I cannot let a cheap card (5,6,7,8) go from the Landlord\'s hand.\n\nMy hand analysis, however, has so many good cards! The Red Joker is tempting. But if I can use my Ace to do what I want, I can protect my ace and save the Red Joker. The Landlord\'s remaining cards are somewhat known (Black Joker, 2s, A, K). If I play a high card, the Landlord must spend a high card or pass.\n\nI am now between a Rock and a hard place. Do I let my partner win quickly and pass, which means the Landlord can then play a cheap card and regain board control? No, that is not smart. The correct course is to play the highest card that will achieve this goal. I must choose between the Ace or the Red Joker. The Ace is the correct play. If my teammate has the Black Joker or another bomb, I could make a strong play and still keep resources. Playing Ace forces the Landlord to respond. The Landlord has two cards that could counter this, but the odds are they will have to play a high card, which will give me the upper hand.\n\nSo, the optimal play is to play my Ace.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: My teammate (Previous Player) has only 4 cards left and has led a very low single card (♠4). As the player acting immediately after my teammate and before the Landlord (Next Player), my role is to "gatekeep" and ensure the Landlord cannot win the trick with a low or medium card. I have a strong hand with high cards (Red Joker, A, K). Playing a small card would allow the Landlord to play a slightly higher card (like a 10 or J) to take the lead cheaply. Playing the Ace exerts maximum pressure, forcing the Landlord to use a 2 or a Joker (their highest cards) or pass, thereby protecting my teammate\'s potential win condition.\n\nReason: My teammate is in a winning position (4 cards). Leading a 4 suggests they want to pass the turn or force out high cards. Since the Landlord plays after me, I must play a high card to block the Landlord from sliding in a cheap win. The Ace is the best candidate as it is very high but saves the Red Joker for an absolute emergency. This forces the Landlord to spend a top-tier card or surrender control.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♥A"]}'}
